XSPECIAL NOTES FOR HACK:
X
XLUCK:
X
XLuck is used in many different features of hack. In particular, luck
Xis used in combat to determine whether the user and his opponent hit, and
Xhow much damage is done. Luck is also used when praying, and in using
Xwands of wishing.
X
XLuck begins at 0, and increases or decreases as the game goes on. The
Xhigher the user's luck, the luckier he is. The maximum luck is usually 10
X(although this changes with the phases of the moon in some versions). The
Xminimum luck is -10.
X
XThe user should try to be aware of two basic ways of modifying his luck.
XThe first is that killing any tame creature (such as your dog), and killing
Xany human (such as a shopkeeper) will result in the user's luck being
Xdrastically reduced. The second is that if the user throws a gem at a
Xunicorn, his luck will be increased. It is the latter that we will discuss
Xin depth.
X
XFirst, you must have an unidentified gem to get an increase of one, or
Xan identified gem to get an increase of five. More importantly, it is
Xfairly difficult to get into a position to throw a gem at a unicorn, since
Xthe unicorn will never move into such a space. Should the unicorn be
Xforced to move into such a space, it will teleport to another space on the
Xlevel.
X
XThere are numerous methods to circumvent this. One method is to quaff
Xa haste potion, and simply walk next to it during the user's "free" move.
XAnother, more more useful method, is to get a teleport control ring and
Xteleport next to the unicorn (or along a line of sight). The unicorn does
Xnot have a chance to move away, since teleporting if figured after the
Xunicorn moves. The latter method is more common, since teleportation
Xis (can be) intrinsic to the character, and teleport control is a necessity.
XOne method to trap the unicorn, so that the user can move next to it
Xwithout the use of ^T is the following:
X
XUnicorns in rooms only move diagnolly, and will only teleport if a move
Xis impossible. Hence you can restrict the unicorn to two spaces as shown
Xbelow:
X
X-------
X|.....|
X|..@..+
X|1....|
X|.2...|
X-------
X
XThe unicorn will only move in the spaces marked 1 and 2, and will not
Xteleport away. This allows the user to teleport freely next to the unicorn.
X
XTHE LITTLE DOG:
X
XTo me, the most interesting (although by far not the most useful) feature
Xof hack is the little dog, and the way tame creatures work in general in hack.
XIf the user wishes to try to make use of his pet, he should be aware of its
Xabilities, limitations and its intelligence (or lack thereof).
X
XThe dog is friendly towards the user, and will tend to follow him around
Xthe dungeon. However, the dog, like the user, requires food to live. The dog
Xcan eat every non-poisonous substance safely, and will not eat tainted meat.
XUnder normal circumstances, the dog will not eat food rations, fruit and
Xother "human" foods.
X
XThe dog goes through three stages of development: little dog, dog and
Xlarge dog. In fact, the dog actually goes through more, but they are divided
Xequally into these three classes. All dogs may attack twice per turn, and move
Xtwo spaces per turn (i.e. they are hasted). Dogs will do about 1-6 points of
Xdamage per hit, so they can do 2-12 points of damage per round. A hasted dog
Xcan be quite vicious.
X
XThe dog improves by gaining experience from killing monsters, just as the
Xcharacter does. The dog, however, is limited to level 6, and about 85 hp, so
Xthe dog cannot become as powerful as the character. However, initially,
Xthe dog can be much better than the character at killing creatures, and so can
Xbe useful for low-level characters as a guard dog.
X
XIf the user goes up or down levels for any reason, then if the dog is on
Xa space adjacent to the character, he will change levels with the user. But
Xif the dog is not on an adjacent space, he is stranded on the old level.
XWhen a dog becomes stranded and the hacker does not return for a while, he
Xbecomes wild. The user may also find other wild dogs in the dungeon. A wild
Xdog will attack the user like any other monster. However, the dog will be
Xtamed if the user throws any food (except fruit) at the dog. The dog will then
X"devour" the food, and is from then on the pet of the user. Note, also, that
Xthe user is not limited to one pet, but may tame as many pets as he wishes.
X
XA pet has other abilities as well. The first is that the pet will tend
Xnot to walk over cursed items unless the item has food on it which the dog
Xintends to eat. This gives the user a cheap test to see if an item is cursed.
XIn addition, the dog will pick up items that he finds, and bring them to the
Xuser.
X
XFinally, the dog will stay closer to the user if the user has a tripe
Xration.
X
XGHOST LEVELS:
X
XOccasionally, when a character dies, hack will save the level he died
Xon for future use. This level is called a ghost level, and the reader is
Xbound to find one sooner or later. Everything on the level will be exactly
Xas it was when the character died, with one addition. The old character's
Xghost is also on the level. Ghosts, although hard to kill, can do almost no
Xdamage. The nice thing about ghost levels is that all of the old character's
Xitems are in one pile on the space the character died. It is a simple matter
Xto get these items. But be warned; most of these items will be cursed.
XIn fact, the hardest thing about ghost levels is that usually whatever
Xkilled the old character is powerful enough to kill the new character as
Xwell. There can be multiple ghost on one level. I recall one level
Xwhich had three cockatrices and five ghosts.
X
XFortunately, ghost levels are eventually cleared by the program after
Xa certain period of time, so the user does not have to worry to much about
Xthem in general.
X
XWISHING FOR OBJECTS:
X
XNear the end of the game, the user will find wands of wishing. When
Xthese are zapped, the user is asked to wish for an item. One of the most
Xfrustrating things in hack is when a beginning player can not figure out
Xwhat the exact name of the item he wants to wish for is. Here is how wishing
Xis accomplished.
X
XIn the previous item list, the name of the object is the name the user
Xgives hack to wish for that item, not including the number wished for, and
Xthe plus of the item (when wishing for weapons or armor). The general
Xformat is:
X
X<# wished for><plus of item><name of item>
X
XThe user can NEVER wish for more than 3 of any item, or for an item with
Xmagical plus greater than +3.
X
XExample: To wish for 3 potions of gain level, the user would type "3 potions of gain level". To wish for a +3 elven cloak, "+3 elven cloak".
X
XNote that if the program can't understand what you want, it will make
Xa random selection.
X
